---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { pillars, foundations, tests, issues, roadmap, refs } from '../data/content.js';
---
<Base title="Fix the Foundations | Australian Digital Regulation Review" description="Independent public-interest review of the Online Safety Amendment and Australia's digital regulatory foundations.">
  <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

  <header class="mast">
    <div class="wrap identity">
      <div class="mark" aria-hidden="true"><span>★</span><b>AU</b></div>
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">INDEPENDENT PUBLIC INTEREST REVIEW</div>
        <div class="agency">Australian Digital Regulation Review</div>
        <div class="subagency">Freedom • Innovation • Enterprise • Accountable Government</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <nav aria-label="Primary navigation">
    <div class="wrap nav">
      <a href="#position">Our position</a>
      <a href="#amendment">The amendment</a>
      <a href="#foundations">Fix first</a>
      <a href="#roadmap">Reform sequence</a>
      <a href="#sources">Evidence</a>
    </div>
  </nav>

  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero" id="position">
      <div class="wrap hero-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow gold">PUBLIC INTEREST PAPER • VERSION 3 • NOVEMBER 2026</div>
          <h1>Fix the foundations<br /><span>before expanding the power.</span></h1>
          <p class="lead">Do not proceed with the Online Safety Amendment in its current form. Australia should pause, repair its fragmented digital regulatory foundations, rebuild public trust and then prove which additional powers are genuinely necessary.</p>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="btn" href="#amendment">Why we should pause</a>
            <a class="textlink" href="#roadmap">See the six-stage alternative →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <aside class="brief">
          <div>THE CENTRAL TEST</div>
          <blockquote>Would we still grant this power if it were exercised by the government we trust least?</blockquote>
          <p>Good institutions do not require perfect officials. They remain trustworthy when people are mistaken, compromised, politically motivated or corrupt.</p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="decision-strip">
      <div class="wrap decision-grid">
        <div><span>RECOMMENDATION</span><strong>Pause the amendment</strong></div>
        <p>Protecting children and addressing serious online harm are legitimate public goals. Urgency does not remove the obligation to prove necessity, proportionality, effectiveness and the absence of a less intrusive alternative.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" aria-label="Review values">
      <div class="wrap pillars">
        {pillars.map(([heading, text]) => (
          <article><h3>{heading}</h3><p>{text}</p></article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" id="amendment">
      <div class="kicker">01 • THE IMMEDIATE PROBLEM</div>
      <h2>A significant new power on foundations that already need renovation</h2>
      <p class="intro">The problem is not the stated safety objective. The problem is adding a broad new duty, enforcement architecture and information expectations before Australia has mapped existing authority, removed overlap, secured citizen data and guaranteed meaningful review.</p>

      <div class="position-card">
        <div><b>OUR POSITION</b><h3>Do not proceed in the current form.</h3></div>
        <p>Return only after an independent foundation review demonstrates what existing law cannot achieve and identifies the precise residual power required.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="issue-grid">
        {issues.map(([heading, text], index) => (
          <article>
            <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <h3>{heading}</h3>
            <p>{text}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="dark" id="foundations">
      <div class="wrap section">
        <div class="kicker pale">02 • RENOVATE BEFORE EXTENDING</div>
        <h2>Repair the institutional foundations first</h2>
        <p class="intro pale">Australia should be able to protect people online without normalising duplicated bureaucracy, broad data access, weak contestability or a whole-of-citizen view.</p>
        <div class="foundation-list">
          {foundations.map(([heading, text], index) => (
            <article>
              <b>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</b>
              <div><h3>{heading}</h3><p>{text}</p></div>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" id="burden">
      <div class="kicker">03 • SHIFT THE BURDEN OF PROOF</div>
      <h2>Government must justify intervention</h2>
      <p class="intro">Citizens should not have to prove why government should leave lawful activity alone. Before creating significant digital power, government should answer these questions publicly.</p>
      <div class="test-table" role="list">
        {tests.map(([heading, text], index) => (
          <div role="listitem"><b>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</b><strong>{heading}</strong><p>{text}</p></div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="comparison">
      <div class="wrap section">
        <div class="kicker">04 • CHANGE THE LEGISLATIVE INSTINCT</div>
        <h2>Replace reaction with disciplined reform</h2>
        <div class="compare-grid">
          <article class="bad">
            <span>CURRENT INSTINCT</span>
            <h3>Concern → pressure → new power</h3>
            <ol>
              <li>High-profile harm</li><li>Media and political pressure</li><li>Rapid legislation</li><li>New regulator, report or power</li><li>More complexity and data collection</li><li>The next problem triggers another layer</li>
            </ol>
          </article>
          <article class="good">
            <span>BETTER APPROACH</span>
            <h3>Evidence → repair → necessity</h3>
            <ol>
              <li>Define and measure the harm</li><li>Map existing laws and powers</li><li>Identify implementation failure</li><li>Remove duplicated administration</li><li>Test rights, cost and alternatives</li><li>Legislate only the remaining gap</li>
            </ol>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" id="roadmap">
      <div class="kicker">05 • THE SIX-STAGE ALTERNATIVE</div>
      <h2>Pause. Map. Consolidate. Protect. Rebuild trust. Justify.</h2>
      <div class="roadmap">
        {roadmap.map(([number, heading, text]) => (
          <article><b>{number}</b><div><h3>{heading}</h3><p>{text}</p></div></article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="architecture">
      <div class="wrap section">
        <div class="kicker">06 • PRIVACY-PRESERVING GOVERNMENT</div>
        <h2>Integrated service without an integrated citizen profile</h2>
        <p class="intro">One front door for the citizen. Federated authoritative systems behind it. No routine browsing, no routine export and no AI-determined permissions.</p>
        <div class="flow">
          <div><strong>Authorised case</strong><small>purpose + legislation</small></div><i>→</i>
          <div><strong>Policy gateway</strong><small>role + scope + expiry</small></div><i>→</i>
          <div><strong>Federated source</strong><small>tax / health / identity</small></div><i>→</i>
          <div><strong>Minimum answer</strong><small>yes / no / attribute</small></div><i>→</i>
          <div><strong>Independent audit</strong><small>immutable log + review</small></div>
        </div>
        <blockquote class="rule">Government data should be searched to answer an authorised question, not searched to discover citizens worth investigating.</blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="service">
      <div class="wrap section">
        <div class="kicker">07 • REBUILD TRUST</div>
        <h2>Authority is a responsibility to serve, not a privilege to command.</h2>
        <div class="columns">
          <div><h3>Customer-service posture</h3><p>Plain language, one front door, tell-us-once administration, assistance before punitive enforcement for honest mistakes and published service levels.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Review as a right</h3><p>Reasons, correction, human reconsideration, accessible Ombudsman review, merits review where appropriate and preserved judicial review.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Government meets the standard</h3><p>Publish cyber maturity, privacy failures, access misuse, response times, reversal rates, compliance cost and the realised benefits of restructures and programs.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="final-call">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="kicker pale">THE QUESTION PARLIAMENT SHOULD ANSWER LAST</div>
        <h2>What additional power is actually necessary?</h2>
        <p>That answer should emerge after the foundations are repaired, from evidence and demonstrated public need. It should not be assumed from urgency, regulatory ambition or the political desire to be seen doing something.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" id="sources">
      <div class="kicker">08 • PRIMARY EVIDENCE</div>
      <h2>Review the source material</h2>
      <p class="intro">This is independent commentary, not an Australian Government publication. Facts, interpretation and recommendations should remain visibly distinct.</p>
      <div class="sources">
        {refs.map(([organisation, title, url], index) => (
          <a href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <div><b>{organisation}</b><p>{title}</p></div>
            <i>↗</i>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <div class="wrap">
      <strong>Australian Digital Regulation Review</strong>
      <p>Independent public-interest analysis • Not affiliated with or endorsed by the Australian Government.</p>
      <p class="legal">This site intentionally does not reproduce the Commonwealth Coat of Arms. The Arms signify Commonwealth authority and ownership; third-party use may require written permission.</p>
    </div>
  </footer>
</Base>
